    "['interface [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router ospf 1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco IOS command for the Juniper OSPF interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 2\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. I used OSPF process ID 1 (common default in Cisco)\\n2. The area number is converted from Juniper's dotted format (0.0.0.2) to Cisco's integer format (2)\\n3. The network statement uses the subnet address (192.168.2.0) and wildcard mask (0.0.0.255) derived from the interface IP (192.168.2.2/24)\\n4. Cisco requires the network statement under the OSPF process rather than interface-specific configuration like Juniper\", 'trans_template': None}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 2', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 2', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco IOS command for the Juniper OSPF interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 2\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. I used OSPF process ID 1 (common default in Cisco)\\n2. The area number is converted from Juniper's dotted format (0.0.0.2) to Cisco's integer format (2)\\n3. The network statement uses the subnet address (192.168.2.0) and wildcard mask (0.0.0.255) derived from the interface IP (192.168.2.2/24)\\n4. Cisco requires the network statement under the OSPF process rather than interface-specific configuration like Juniper\", 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
    "['interface [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router ospf 1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper OSPF interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 2\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Cisco, OSPF is enabled with a process ID (1 in this case)\\n2. The \"network\" command specifies:\\n   - The interface\\'s network (172.17.1.0 derived from ge-0/0/1\\'s IP)\\n   - Wildcard mask (0.0.0.255 for /24)\\n   - Area number (2, since 0.0.0.2 in Juniper is area 2 in Cisco)\\n3. Note that Cisco uses simple area numbers while Juniper uses dotted notation, but they represent the same area', 'trans_template': None}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 2', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 2', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper OSPF interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 2\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Cisco, OSPF is enabled with a process ID (1 in this case)\\n2. The \"network\" command specifies:\\n   - The interface\\'s network (172.17.1.0 derived from ge-0/0/1\\'s IP)\\n   - Wildcard mask (0.0.0.255 for /24)\\n   - Area number (2, since 0.0.0.2 in Juniper is area 2 in Cisco)\\n3. Note that Cisco uses simple area numbers while Juniper uses dotted notation, but they represent the same area', 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
